{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\fs22  U.S. Capital Private Bank Knowledge Base\line \line Title:  Understanding Consideration in Contract Law\line \line  Overview\line \line In the world of contract law, consideration is an essential component that validates the agreement between two or more parties. Whether you're asked to pay an upfront fee or offer something of value for an engagement, it's crucial to understand when such requests are legally validand when they are not.\line \line This article explains what consideration means under the law, how it applies in real-world contract scenarios, and what to watch out for when you're entering into any agreement.\line \line  What Is Consideration?\line \line Consideration is the exchange of value between parties to a contract. It can be:\line \line -\line A promise to do something you're not legally required to do.\line \line -\line A promise not to do something you legally could do (such as filing a lawsuit).\line \line This exchange must reflect a bargained-for detriment, meaning both parties give something up or assume a responsibility in return for a benefit.\line \line  Example: You damage a neighbor's golf cart. Instead of suing, they agree not to take legal action if you pay $1,000. Your payment and their forbearance represent mutual consideration.\line \line  Legal Examples\line \line  "WITNESSETH that: Grantor, for and in consideration of the sum of TWENTY-ONE THOUSAND DOLLARS has and by these presents does grant, bargain, sell..."\line \line This is a typical phrase found in legal documents, affirming that something of measurable value has changed hands.\line \line  When Is a Contract Unenforceable?\line \line Even if there's an agreement, a contract without valid consideration is not enforceable. Below are key scenarios:\line \line  Invalid Contracts May Involve\line  Example\line \line Lack of Consideration\line A gift not supported by legal value.\line \line Duress or Coercion\line Forcing someone to sign under threat.\line \line Misrepresentation or Fraud\line Withholding material facts.\line \line Unconscionability\line One-sided, shockingly unfair terms.\line \line Public Policy Violations\line A contract for an illegal purpose.\line \line Mental Incapacity or Minority\line A 17-year-old contracting without guardian consent.\line \line  Common Contract Myths\line \line  Myth: Any promise or agreement counts as a contract.\line \line  Truth: Without legal consideration, a written or verbal promise may be deemed a gift, not a contract.\line \line  Myth: All fees required to enter into an agreement are lawful.\line \line  Truth: Verification fees may be valid if they represent lawful considerationbut operating or transactional fees must be explicitly supported by documented, enforceable services or obligations.\line \line  Legal Precedents & Key Cases\line \line -\line Thomas v Thomas (1842): Consideration must have legal value, not emotional promises.\line \line -\line Chappell v Nestle (1960): Consideration must be sufficient, but need not be adequate.\line \line -\line Tweddle v Atkinson (1861): Consideration must move from the promisee.\line \line -\line Williams v Roffey Bros (1990): Courts may infer consideration from practical benefit.\line \line -\line Currie v Misa (1875): Defined consideration as a benefit to one party or detriment to another.\line \line  Capacity & Competency: Who Can Contract?\line \line  Minors: Cannot be bound unless its for necessities.\line  Mental Incapacity: A contract is voidable if the person cant comprehend its nature.\line  Intoxication: May void a contract only if the sober party exploited the condition.\line  Duress/Undue Influence: Voidable if coercion or exploitation is present.\line \line  Key Takeaways\line \line -\line  All parties in a contract must give and receive something of value.\line \line -\line  Promises without legal consideration are not enforceable.\line \line -\line  You cannot enforce a contract for something illegal or against public policy.\line \line -\line  Contracts with people lacking legal capacity are generally voidable.\line \line  Need a Deeper Dive?\line \line  Watch our official explainer video:\line  Understanding Consideration in Contract Law  U.S. Capital Private Bank\line \line  U.S. Capital Private Bank\line Providing clarity for clients in every legal interaction.\line Last updated: July 2025\line \line Thank you,\line US Capital Private Bank Client Relations}